      <p>Ask an owner how long it takes to close the books at the end of a day, and the honest answer is often &ldquo;longer than it should.&rdquo; Not because the math is hard &ndash; because the numbers live in three or four different places, and someone has to manually pull them together before anything adds up.</p>

      <h2>Where the time actually goes</h2>
      <p>It&rsquo;s rarely the arithmetic that&rsquo;s slow. It&rsquo;s the reconstruction: which bookings were completed today, which payments match which bookings, which ones were only partially paid, and which branch each of those belongs to. When that information is spread across a booking log, a payment log, and someone&rsquo;s memory of who paid cash, reconciliation turns into detective work.</p>

      <h2>The one-record principle</h2>
      <p>The fix is structural, not procedural: every payment should be recorded against the booking it belongs to, at the moment it happens &ndash; not batched at the end of the day from memory or a paper slip. When the payment and the booking are the same record, reconciliation stops being a separate task. The numbers are already correct because they were never allowed to drift apart.</p>
      <p>This is the core idea behind <a href="/features/payment-tracking/">Zennly&rsquo;s payment tracking</a> &ndash; every payment logs the total amount and settlement status at the point of service, so the daily close is a read, not a rebuild.</p>

      <h2>What &ldquo;fast&rdquo; actually looks like</h2>
      <p>With a single source of truth per branch, a daily close should take under a minute per branch &ndash; open the dashboard, confirm the total matches what&rsquo;s in the till, done. For a four-branch business, that&rsquo;s the difference between an hour of cross-checking every morning and a five-minute review before the day starts.</p>
      <p>If your reconciliation still takes longer than your last service of the day, the process is the bottleneck &ndash; not your team.</p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <h3>What is daily revenue reconciliation?</h3>
      <p>It is the end-of-day check that bookings, services delivered and payments received (cash, card and online) all agree for each branch.</p>
      <h3>How long should daily reconciliation take?</h3>
      <p>When payments are recorded against each booking as they happen, the close is a quick check rather than a morning of counting slips and chats. Our <a href="/daily-close-checklist/">free daily close checklist</a> lists the twelve steps.</p>
      <h3>Can I reconcile several branches at once?</h3>
      <p>Yes. With one account and one dashboard across all branches, the owner sees every branch&#x27;s day together.</p>
      
